1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,429
Coach: Now prepare a short account of an experience.

2
00:00:03,429 --> 00:00:06,428
Coach: An ordinary contribution can be worth explaining;

3
00:00:06,428 --> 00:00:09,428
Coach: you do not need a dramatic achievement.

4
00:00:09,428 --> 00:00:12,000
Coach: Choose something you can describe truthfully,

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,714
Coach: or use the labelled fictional guide case purely for language practice.

6
00:00:16,714 --> 00:00:20,571
Coach: Do not present that case as your own biography.

7
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:26,064
Coach: For a real experience, you may omit names or other private identifiers.

8
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:27,778
Coach: Keep the substance accurate.

9
00:00:27,778 --> 00:00:31,207
Coach: If the topic feels too personal, choose another.

10
00:00:31,207 --> 00:00:35,921
Coach: The example facts remain available for anyone using the fictional case;

11
00:00:35,921 --> 00:00:40,635
Coach: they are a reference, not an answer copied into your space.

12
00:00:40,985 --> 00:00:43,985
Coach: Ask yourself which part the listener needs:

13
00:00:43,985 --> 00:00:46,556
Coach: the situation, your contribution, a decision,

14
00:00:46,556 --> 00:00:49,128
Coach: an observed result, or a reflection.

15
00:00:49,128 --> 00:00:55,128
Coach: You can use these distinctions to plan, but they are not a required sequence.

16
00:00:55,478 --> 00:00:59,335
Coach: Keep your own work separate from other people’s contributions.

17
00:00:59,335 --> 00:01:04,907
Coach: In the guide case, drawing and changing the map belong to the participant;

18
00:01:04,907 --> 00:01:08,764
Coach: listing the times and assembling the guide do not.

19
00:01:08,764 --> 00:01:11,764
Coach: Sharing credit makes the account more precise

20
00:01:11,764 --> 00:01:13,907
Coach: without erasing the participant’s work.

21
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:19,400
Coach: Privately identify one detail you know and one boundary you should retain.

22
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:25,400
Coach: It might be an unmeasured outcome or a future change you have not made.

23
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,971
Coach: If you use the fictional case,

24
00:01:27,971 --> 00:01:33,114
Coach: keep its one observed visitor separate from any claim about everyone else.

25
00:01:41,464 --> 00:01:43,607
Coach: Rehearse your account in English.

26
00:01:43,607 --> 00:01:47,464
Coach: Say it aloud, write privately, or think it through.

27
00:01:47,464 --> 00:01:50,036
Coach: You may pause or change topics.

28
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:52,607
Coach: This player does not record you.

29
00:01:52,607 --> 00:01:55,178
Coach: If you use the fictional example,

30
00:01:55,178 --> 00:02:00,750
Coach: keep it labelled as practice rather than presenting it as your own experience.

31
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:04,529
Coach: Take some time to put the account together.

32
00:02:04,529 --> 00:02:07,957
Coach: In Watch, a model follows the thinking pause.

33
00:02:07,957 --> 00:02:12,672
Coach: In Practice, choose the model when you want to compare approaches,

34
00:02:12,672 --> 00:02:14,815
Coach: or skip it and continue.

35
00:02:14,815 --> 00:02:17,386
Coach: You can return for another attempt.

36
00:02:52,736 --> 00:02:56,165
You: I drew the map for a visitor guide.

37
00:02:56,165 --> 00:03:00,450
You: Maya listed the session times, and Noah assembled the guide.

38
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:07,308
You: The day before the event, my map said Studio B but the schedule said Blue Room.

39
00:03:07,308 --> 00:03:09,450
You: I checked with the coordinator:

40
00:03:09,450 --> 00:03:14,593
You: both names meant the same room, with Blue Room on the door.

41
00:03:14,593 --> 00:03:18,022
You: I changed my map and told the others.

42
00:03:18,022 --> 00:03:22,736
You: I later saw one visitor use it to find the room.

43
00:03:22,736 --> 00:03:29,165
You: We did not survey visitors, so I cannot say how much the guide helped overall.

44
00:03:29,515 --> 00:03:34,229
Maya: What would you do differently if you were preparing another guide?

45
00:03:34,579 --> 00:03:39,722
Coach: A possible reply would propose agreeing the room names earlier next time.

46
00:03:39,722 --> 00:03:44,865
Coach: Connect that idea to the mismatch, and keep it in the future:

47
00:03:44,865 --> 00:03:48,293
Coach: the case has not tested the new approach.

48
00:03:48,293 --> 00:03:52,151
Coach: You can study this account without copying its events

49
00:03:52,151 --> 00:03:54,722
Coach: or wording into your own experience.

50
00:03:55,072 --> 00:03:58,072
Coach: For any account you choose to review,

51
00:03:58,072 --> 00:04:04,072
Coach: check whether your contribution is clear and the result words match what you know.

52
00:04:04,072 --> 00:04:10,929
Coach: If you have not prepared an account, use the supplied example to examine those two points,

53
00:04:10,929 --> 00:04:15,215
Coach: then return to your own experience when you are ready.

54
00:04:15,565 --> 00:04:19,422
Coach: For a follow-up about what you would change,

55
00:04:19,422 --> 00:04:22,851
Coach: distinguish a future intention from something already done.

56
00:04:22,851 --> 00:04:27,994
Coach: For a question about evidence, stay with the observation you can support.

57
00:04:27,994 --> 00:04:33,994
Coach: You can acknowledge a missing detail instead of constructing a confident answer around it.

58
00:04:34,344 --> 00:04:36,915
Coach: Choose one part to revise privately:

59
00:04:36,915 --> 00:04:42,916
Coach: clarify your role, narrow a result claim, or mark a future plan as future.

60
00:04:42,916 --> 00:04:45,058
Coach: You decide what needs attention.

61
00:04:45,058 --> 00:04:49,773
Coach: No revision is required merely because the model used different words

62
00:04:49,773 --> 00:04:51,487
Coach: or a different experience.

63
00:05:03,837 --> 00:05:07,266
Coach: Choose relevant details and keep their limits intact.

64
00:05:07,266 --> 00:05:09,837
Coach: You can return for another attempt,

65
00:05:09,837 --> 00:05:14,980
Coach: using your own truthful experience or explicitly labelled fiction for language practice.
